// **a bool is not a width, so only 0 and 1 name one.** those two are what `echo` prints for
// `true` and `false`; expressions/bool_from_zero_and_one is the conversion. everything else
// used to compile and print 0, because a `bool` destination retyped the literal and the value
// then came back out by string-comparing the token to "true".
//
// only a *written* literal is refused. `bool $b = $n;` on an int32 still converts at runtime,
// exactly as `int32 $t = $f;` still truncates - a literal is a compile-time question and a variable
// is a narrowing, which is the split every other precision refusal already makes

bool $a = 3;

// and the mirror: there is no number `true` means
int32 $k = true;

echo $a;
echo $k;

// a bool return is a destination too - stripping the hint because can_type_a_literal
// refuses bool compiled this as truthiness (`icmp ne 0`)
function bad() : bool
{
    return 3;
}
